WEBVTT

1
00:00:01.199 --> 00:00:03.359
So we know now how to create a character

2
00:00:03.359 --> 00:00:04.679
and have them respond to input

3
00:00:04.679 --> 00:00:06.280
and have them animate as they do so.

4
00:00:06.280 --> 00:00:07.259
But what is the glue

5
00:00:07.259 --> 00:00:09.640
that binds all of these classes together?

6
00:00:09.640 --> 00:00:13.589
Let's take a look at how we set up a world in Unreal.

7
00:00:13.589 --> 00:00:14.769
There are two main classes

8
00:00:14.769 --> 00:00:17.859
that handle what game is being played.

9
00:00:17.859 --> 00:00:19.120
Firstly, we have the game mode

10
00:00:19.120 --> 00:00:21.600
and this handles the rules for the current game.

11
00:00:21.600 --> 00:00:24.359
That could be how many players are allowed to connect,

12
00:00:24.359 --> 00:00:25.879
where do these players spawn

13
00:00:25.879 --> 00:00:28.559
and how many points will earn a team a win.

14
00:00:28.559 --> 00:00:30.120
The game mode is the intended home

15
00:00:30.120 --> 00:00:31.719
for this kind of logic.

16
00:00:31.719 --> 00:00:34.600
And in a networked game, the game mode is server only

17
00:00:34.600 --> 00:00:37.820
because we need it to be authoritative.

18
00:00:37.820 --> 00:00:39.780
The game state on the other hand

19
00:00:39.780 --> 00:00:40.979
is a replicated actor

20
00:00:40.979 --> 00:00:43.100
that contains all of the pertinent information

21
00:00:43.100 --> 00:00:45.679
about the set game mode.

22
00:00:45.679 --> 00:00:47.939
Let's take capture the flag as an example.

23
00:00:47.939 --> 00:00:50.399
The game mode would handle actually capturing the flag

24
00:00:50.399 --> 00:00:52.340
and the allocation of the points.

25
00:00:52.340 --> 00:00:54.479
Those points are then stored in the game state

26
00:00:54.479 --> 00:00:57.429
where any client can easily see them.

27
00:00:57.429 --> 00:00:59.950
There's also a third critical class that we can implement

28
00:00:59.950 --> 00:01:02.619
and that is the game instance.

29
00:01:02.619 --> 00:01:04.379
While the game mode and game state

30
00:01:04.379 --> 00:01:07.099
are destroyed and recreated between levels,

31
00:01:07.099 --> 00:01:10.500
think of the score beam reset when we move to a new map,

32
00:01:10.500 --> 00:01:11.980
the game instance is created

33
00:01:11.980 --> 00:01:14.239
as soon as the game EXE is executed

34
00:01:14.239 --> 00:01:17.349
and persists across all maps.

35
00:01:17.349 --> 00:01:19.189
We use the game instance to store data

36
00:01:19.189 --> 00:01:21.750
that we want to transfer between levels.

37
00:01:21.750 --> 00:01:23.590
Building on our capture the flag example,

38
00:01:23.590 --> 00:01:26.829
so we pick the red team in the main menu.

39
00:01:26.829 --> 00:01:28.969
When we load into the actual gameplay map,

40
00:01:28.969 --> 00:01:30.689
we'd want the game mode to assign you

41
00:01:30.689 --> 00:01:32.670
to the team of your choosing.

42
00:01:33.189 --> 00:01:34.469
As the game instance persists,

43
00:01:34.469 --> 00:01:37.189
we can safely bring that data along from the main menu

44
00:01:37.189 --> 00:01:40.260
over into the main level.

45
00:01:40.260 --> 00:01:42.420
The game mode gives us exceptionally useful

46
00:01:42.420 --> 00:01:44.019
virtual functions to implement,

47
00:01:44.019 --> 00:01:45.780
allowing us to shape any kind of game mode

48
00:01:45.780 --> 00:01:47.859
that we would choose.

49
00:01:47.859 --> 00:01:49.459
The game mode is the default class

50
00:01:49.459 --> 00:01:51.299
that comes with the engine as a 4.14

51
00:01:51.299 --> 00:01:54.540
and is a more streamlined version of game mode base

52
00:01:54.540 --> 00:01:55.980
with a lot of the convenience functions

53
00:01:55.980 --> 00:01:59.650
we'd expect of most standard multiplayer modes.

54
00:01:59.650 --> 00:02:02.370
As an example, we have the init game function

55
00:02:02.370 --> 00:02:05.609
and this is called exceptionally early in the game flow.

56
00:02:05.609 --> 00:02:07.090
This is our initial kickoff

57
00:02:07.090 --> 00:02:08.090
and it's called even before

58
00:02:08.090 --> 00:02:10.370
the pre-initialize components function.

59
00:02:10.370 --> 00:02:11.770
It's used by the game mode base

60
00:02:11.770 --> 00:02:15.849
to initialize parameters and spawn any helper classes.

61
00:02:15.849 --> 00:02:17.629
Additionally, we have pre-login

62
00:02:17.629 --> 00:02:20.629
and this is called as new players request to join the game

63
00:02:20.629 --> 00:02:22.310
and it's ultimately in charge of accepting

64
00:02:22.310 --> 00:02:24.560
or rejecting that connection.

65
00:02:24.560 --> 00:02:27.639
It's called before the proper full fledged login function

66
00:02:27.639 --> 00:02:30.860
with a potentially non-trivial time in between the two,

67
00:02:30.860 --> 00:02:32.180
especially if the joining player

68
00:02:32.219 --> 00:02:35.699
has a lot of content to download before joining.

69
00:02:35.699 --> 00:02:37.039
We also have post login

70
00:02:37.039 --> 00:02:38.960
and this is called after a successful login

71
00:02:38.960 --> 00:02:40.580
and it's the first place it's safe to call

72
00:02:40.580 --> 00:02:42.460
any replicated or networked functionality

73
00:02:42.460 --> 00:02:44.409
on the joined client.

74
00:02:44.409 --> 00:02:45.889
We've got a nice convenience function here

75
00:02:45.889 --> 00:02:48.229
called on post login that can be hooked up in blueprint

76
00:02:48.229 --> 00:02:49.789
for a designer to expand.

77
00:02:49.789 --> 00:02:52.069
So say we want to add some kind of UI sting

78
00:02:52.069 --> 00:02:55.580
to the screen saying someone has joined the game.

79
00:02:55.580 --> 00:02:58.099
Next, we've got the handle starting new player function

80
00:02:58.099 --> 00:03:00.740
and this is called after post login.

81
00:03:00.740 --> 00:03:02.500
By default, it spawns the pawn in

82
00:03:02.500 --> 00:03:04.319
but we could extend it to do anything we want to,

83
00:03:04.319 --> 00:03:06.699
say, changing the mesh of a particular player

84
00:03:06.699 --> 00:03:10.090
or assigning them to a team.

85
00:03:10.090 --> 00:03:12.370
Next, we've got spawn default pawn at transform

86
00:03:12.370 --> 00:03:13.210
and this is a function

87
00:03:13.210 --> 00:03:16.289
that actually does the spawning of that pawn.

88
00:03:16.289 --> 00:03:17.770
And finally, we've got logout

89
00:03:17.770 --> 00:03:19.330
and this is called when a player leaves the game

90
00:03:19.330 --> 00:03:21.349
or is destroyed.

91
00:03:21.349 --> 00:03:23.069
On logout is called in blueprint.

92
00:03:23.069 --> 00:03:25.870
So say we want a player has left the game UI prompt

93
00:03:25.870 --> 00:03:29.110
to show on the screen.

94
00:03:29.110 --> 00:03:31.270
Any number of classes extending off of game mode

95
00:03:31.270 --> 00:03:33.469
can be created all with different rules.

96
00:03:33.469 --> 00:03:35.349
So your classic capture the flag, death match

97
00:03:35.590 --> 00:03:37.310
and search and destroy would all be game modes

98
00:03:37.310 --> 00:03:38.550
with their own sets of rules

99
00:03:38.550 --> 00:03:41.370
and methods for spawning players in.

100
00:03:41.370 --> 00:03:44.710
When talking about game mode as opposed to game mode base,

101
00:03:44.710 --> 00:03:47.419
we have access to match states.

102
00:03:47.419 --> 00:03:49.919
For example, we have the match state entering map

103
00:03:49.919 --> 00:03:52.620
followed by in progress and waiting to start.

104
00:03:52.620 --> 00:03:54.419
Waiting to start being your classic

105
00:03:54.419 --> 00:03:57.419
waiting for more players to join state.

106
00:03:57.419 --> 00:03:59.259
From there, we've got waiting post match.

107
00:03:59.259 --> 00:04:01.139
Say we want to view the player scores

108
00:04:01.139 --> 00:04:04.009
or vote on the next level to travel to

109
00:04:04.009 --> 00:04:07.719
before proceeding into the leaving map state.

110
00:04:07.719 --> 00:04:09.520
The Unreal Engine goes through a game flow

111
00:04:09.520 --> 00:04:13.280
that determines when the necessary classes are initialized.

112
00:04:13.280 --> 00:04:15.240
Similar to the life cycle of an actor,

113
00:04:15.240 --> 00:04:16.819
how all this flows will depend differing

114
00:04:16.819 --> 00:04:18.480
on whether or not you're starting in the editor

115
00:04:18.480 --> 00:04:21.569
or launching a standalone game.

116
00:04:21.569 --> 00:04:22.769
So the first thing we need to notice

117
00:04:22.769 --> 00:04:24.250
is that when we start from the editor,

118
00:04:24.250 --> 00:04:25.730
we initialize the editor engine

119
00:04:25.730 --> 00:04:28.519
and then we start the engine proper.

120
00:04:28.519 --> 00:04:30.660
It's only once we've hit play in editor button

121
00:04:30.660 --> 00:04:32.660
that we actually create the game instance.

122
00:04:32.660 --> 00:04:33.980
Whereas in a standalone game,

123
00:04:33.980 --> 00:04:35.139
we initialize our game engine

124
00:04:35.139 --> 00:04:38.300
and then create the game instance.

125
00:04:38.300 --> 00:04:39.920
From there, we can see that we differ slightly

126
00:04:39.920 --> 00:04:42.300
in the steps taken after the creation of the game instance.

127
00:04:42.300 --> 00:04:44.220
However, both paths eventually lead

128
00:04:44.220 --> 00:04:47.519
to calling begin play on the world.

129
00:04:47.519 --> 00:04:49.480
All of this information is worth bearing in mind

130
00:04:49.480 --> 00:04:50.660
when you actually come to decide

131
00:04:50.660 --> 00:04:53.389
where to place any of your game logic.

132
00:04:53.389 --> 00:04:55.470
As we can see with the spinning up of the game instance,

133
00:04:55.470 --> 00:04:57.550
it happens exceptionally early on in the process,

134
00:04:57.550 --> 00:04:59.310
whereas the game mode only exists

135
00:04:59.310 --> 00:05:02.870
when the gold is ready to start playing.

136
00:05:02.870 --> 00:05:05.389
The game mode can be set in a few ways.

137
00:05:05.389 --> 00:05:07.089
The first is to set it on a project level

138
00:05:07.129 --> 00:05:08.730
through the game map settings section

139
00:05:08.730 --> 00:05:11.050
in the default engine.ini config file

140
00:05:11.050 --> 00:05:14.959
with your usual ini key pair syntax.

141
00:05:14.959 --> 00:05:17.040
We can also set it on a per level basis

142
00:05:17.040 --> 00:05:19.560
through the world settings tab in the editor.

143
00:05:19.560 --> 00:05:21.120
This is very useful when you want to consider

144
00:05:21.120 --> 00:05:23.480
non-gameplay maps such as the main menu.

145
00:05:23.480 --> 00:05:26.339
We may just want a level with no real gameplay rules,

146
00:05:26.339 --> 00:05:28.560
just a map to load into to act as an entry point

147
00:05:28.560 --> 00:05:31.459
for any player input.

148
00:05:31.459 --> 00:05:33.860
As mentioned previously, the game state is responsible

149
00:05:33.860 --> 00:05:36.459
for keeping track of game mode data.

150
00:05:36.459 --> 00:05:38.019
More specifically, it keeps track

151
00:05:38.579 --> 00:05:39.819
of the data that is intended to be visible

152
00:05:39.819 --> 00:05:42.120
to all connected clients.

153
00:05:42.120 --> 00:05:44.519
That might be the score, the time until the end of the game,

154
00:05:44.519 --> 00:05:45.899
the time since the start of the game,

155
00:05:45.899 --> 00:05:49.240
or even the list of other players in the match.

156
00:05:49.240 --> 00:05:51.319
It's the game state that replicates down to clients,

157
00:05:51.319 --> 00:05:53.399
giving them access to the current state of the game,

158
00:05:53.399 --> 00:05:55.750
not the game mode.

159
00:05:55.750 --> 00:05:57.149
The game state shouldn't, however,

160
00:05:57.149 --> 00:05:59.589
keep track of specific player data.

161
00:05:59.589 --> 00:06:01.129
There's a class specifically for this

162
00:06:01.129 --> 00:06:02.009
called the player state,

163
00:06:02.009 --> 00:06:05.069
which we're gonna touch on in just a moment.

164
00:06:05.069 --> 00:06:07.189
The game state is trivially expandable.

165
00:06:07.189 --> 00:06:08.930
It's basically just a publicly accessible

166
00:06:08.949 --> 00:06:11.129
replicated class that contains data.

167
00:06:11.129 --> 00:06:13.970
So most use cases are to simply add additional variables

168
00:06:13.970 --> 00:06:17.360
depending on the game mode that you're authoring.

169
00:06:17.360 --> 00:06:18.240
Much like the game state,

170
00:06:18.240 --> 00:06:19.600
there's also a player state class

171
00:06:19.600 --> 00:06:22.579
and it's intended for much of the same uses.

172
00:06:22.579 --> 00:06:24.180
This is for storing player data,

173
00:06:24.180 --> 00:06:27.319
say your own score during a death match.

174
00:06:27.319 --> 00:06:28.180
Consider the following.

175
00:06:28.180 --> 00:06:29.579
We have a death flow that results

176
00:06:29.579 --> 00:06:30.779
in the porn being destroyed,

177
00:06:30.779 --> 00:06:32.860
which is a fairly typical flow.

178
00:06:32.860 --> 00:06:34.819
We have our player die and let them get respawned

179
00:06:34.819 --> 00:06:37.500
through the restart player function in the game mode.

180
00:06:37.500 --> 00:06:40.040
But where do we keep their score?

181
00:06:40.040 --> 00:06:41.620
We could keep it in the player controller,

182
00:06:41.620 --> 00:06:43.000
but that doesn't really make much sense

183
00:06:43.000 --> 00:06:44.600
in terms of architecture.

184
00:06:44.600 --> 00:06:47.870
And this is where the player state comes in.

185
00:06:47.870 --> 00:06:50.069
We can add any number of variables to our player state

186
00:06:50.069 --> 00:06:53.519
and have them survive our game's death flow.

187
00:06:53.519 --> 00:06:55.319
The player state is also replicated

188
00:06:55.319 --> 00:06:58.000
and fully supports client to server remote procedure calls

189
00:06:58.000 --> 00:07:00.240
or RPCs because they're ultimately owned

190
00:07:00.240 --> 00:07:01.600
by the player controller.

191
00:07:01.600 --> 00:07:03.500
And this is gonna give us a direct line of communication

192
00:07:03.500 --> 00:07:06.519
from the client to the server.

193
00:07:06.519 --> 00:07:08.600
The player state is set through the game mode.

194
00:07:08.600 --> 00:07:10.360
The game mode has a public member

195
00:07:10.360 --> 00:07:11.720
called player state class.

196
00:07:11.720 --> 00:07:13.160
And it's here that we actually set the class

197
00:07:13.160 --> 00:07:15.360
that we intend to use as the player state.
